    hero: {
      title: 'TUTE CABRERO',
      subtitle: 'Card Game',
    },
    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Tute cabrero',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Tute cabrero is a point-trick elimination game for 3 to 6 players, popular in Argentina and Uruguay. Each deal you aim either to take no tricks or to finish with the highest or lowest point total; middle totals lose. Four losses eliminate a player; the last one or two remaining win. Uses the standard 40-card Spanish deck with follow-suit and optional singing (declaring king-horse for 20 or 40 points after winning a trick).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Tute originated in Italy as tutti and spread to Spain in the 19th century, becoming one of the most popular card games there. Tute cabrero is an individual-play variant for 3 to 6 players, popular in Argentina and Uruguay. It uses the same 40-card Spanish deck and marriage-style point-trick structure as partnership Tute but with elimination (four losses) and a first-hand rule that coins are trumps and the holder of the two of coins leads. The variant is described in Spanish card-game literature and on dedicated Tute Cabrero pages.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Three to six players, each playing for themselves.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '40-card Spanish pack.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Cards',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Counter-clockwise; 8 each (5–6p), 10 each (4p), or 13 each plus one to centre (3p). First hand coins are trumps; later hands last card to mano turned for trump.',
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
